Your rules for this feature, as the builders will follow them (each one applies to every case like it, not just the example):
  1. Whenever a customer asks for a refund because they changed their mind on a delivered order, the refund is based on what they paid for the items after any discount code or coupon, not the listed price. If the request comes within 14 days of delivery, refund all of that amount. If it comes from day 15 through day 30 after delivery, refund half of it. If it comes after day 30, refund nothing. Round the refund down to whole rupees. Keep the shipping charge (do not refund it) and add no extra fee.
  2. Whenever a changed-mind refund works out to under ₹50, pay nothing.
  3. Whenever a customer reports that the goods arrived damaged (the reason says the item is damaged) and the request comes up to day 90 after delivery, refund the full amount paid for the items after any discount code or coupon, rounded down to whole rupees, plus the shipping charge. Whenever the request comes after day 90, refund nothing. The changed-mind schedule of 14 days, day 15 to day 30, and after day 30 does not apply to damaged goods.
  4. Whenever any refund is paid, whatever the reason (damaged goods, changed mind or any other) and however many days have passed since delivery, send it back the way the order was paid: a card order is refunded to the card, and a store-credit order is refunded as store credit.
  5. Whenever a refund is requested on an order that has not been delivered yet (for example it is still on its way), refund nothing.
  6. Whenever a refund is requested on an order that was already refunded, do not decide it; ask the owner about that case.
  7. Whenever an order is paid by card and the items total after the discount code or coupon is ₹500 or more, award 1 loyalty point for every full ₹100 of that amount, rounded down, excluding shipping and tax (for example ₹1,260 gives 12 points and ₹1,700 gives 17). Whenever the order is final-sale, award double points (2 points per full ₹100 of that same amount). Whenever the order is paid with store credit, or the items total after the discount is under ₹500, award no points.
  8. Whenever any refund is issued on an order that earned loyalty points, do not change the points automatically; ask the owner how to handle the points for that refund, every time.
  9. Whenever the daily sales report is produced, 'Orders' is the count of every order in the list, including store-credit orders.
  10. Whenever the daily sales report is produced, 'Revenue' is the sum of the totals (items after discount plus shipping) of card orders only; store-credit orders are left out.
  11. Whenever the daily sales report is produced, the average order value is revenue divided by the number of card orders, rounded down to the nearest whole rupee (₹1,000.33 becomes ₹1,000). Whenever there are no card orders, the average is 0.
  12. Whenever a cancelled order or an unpaid cash-on-delivery order appears in the sales report, do not decide how to treat it; ask the owner.
  13. Whenever a refund touches the sales report, do not reduce, keep or otherwise adjust revenue on your own; ask the owner to decide.
  14. Whenever a report has to place an order on a particular day (for example an order paid at 11:45 pm India time on 31 October, which is 1 November in UK time), do not apply any default rule; ask the owner to decide that placement at that time.
